 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to the amount of\nfunds that may be used per dwelling unit to modernize certain authorities used\nby the housing trust fund corporation",
    "basePrintNo": "S8178",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8178-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1102, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8178\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to the\namount of funds that may be used per dwelling unit to modernize certain\nauthorities used by the housing trust fund corporation\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the Private Housing Finance Law (PHFL) in order to\namend HTFC's governing statute to increase the maximum loan or grant\namount per dwelling unit from $125,000 to $250,000 and to clarify that\nHTFC may make a finding to exceed this cap in certain circumstances.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivision one of Section 1102 of the\nPHFL, as amended by chapter 235 of the laws of 2021, increasing the\nmaximum cap on HTFC payments, grants, and loans from $125,000 to\n$250,000 per dwelling unit and clarifying that HTFC may make a finding\nto exceed this cap in certain circumstances.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHTFC is currently limited to a maximum loan and grant amount of $125,000\nper unit. The current $125,000 per unit cap was set in 2008 and has not\nbeen revised since that time. Over the past decade and a half, land and\nconstruction costs have risen dramatically, driving up the costs of\naffordable housing. To keep pace with the rising costs of housing devel-\nopment, this increase in the per unit maximum is necessary to ensure\nthat HTFC will continue to be able to develop affordable housing across\nNew York State. Existing law also suggests that HTFC may provide addi-\ntional funds in certain circumstances, and this legislation would clari-\nfy such authority.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis HTFC maximum loan increase proposal was included as a departmental\nin 2018. At that time, the increase would have been from $125,000 to\n$175,000.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nN/A\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
